1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,144
Can you show me cherry-pick?

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:14,054
git cherry-pick COMMIT copies the change of one commit onto the current branch, as a new commit.

3
00:00:14,054 --> 00:00:15,113
The classic use:

4
00:00:15,113 --> 00:00:18,422
an important bug fix was made on a feature branch,

5
00:00:18,422 --> 00:00:23,584
and production needs that fix now, without the rest of the unfinished feature.

6
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:29,733
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:30,267 --> 00:00:32,942
Production needs the price fix only:

8
00:00:32,942 --> 00:00:35,468
specials.txt is not on production:

9
00:00:35,468 --> 00:00:37,251
only the fix came along.

10
00:00:37,251 --> 00:00:41,561
The cherry-picked commit has a new ID but the same change:

11
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:44,706
specials.txt is not on production:

12
00:00:44,706 --> 00:00:46,545
only the fix came along.

13
00:00:46,545 --> 00:00:50,989
The cherry-picked commit has a new ID but the same change:

14
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:54,463
Let me try something.

15
00:00:54,463 --> 00:00:57,923
Production also wants the Tuesday special:

16
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:00,229
CONFLICT (modify/delete):

17
00:01:00,229 --> 00:01:03,543
specials.txt deleted in HEAD and modified in …:

18
00:01:03,543 --> 00:01:06,433
the Tuesday commit modifies specials.txt,

19
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:13,130
a file created by an earlier commit ("Start the specials board") that production does not have.

20
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:18,911
A cherry-picked commit carries only its own change, not the commits it depends on.

21
00:01:20,667 --> 00:01:25,971
Abort, and pick the dependency first, in order (a range):

22
00:01:27,733 --> 00:01:28,447
Got it.

23
00:01:28,447 --> 00:01:31,198
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:31,733 --> 00:01:37,038
git cherry-pick C copies one commit's change onto the current branch as a new commit.

25
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:40,978
It does not bring the commits C depends on:

26
00:01:40,978 --> 00:01:43,099
pick those first, in order.

27
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:48,463
Use -x for backports; prefer merging when you want everything.

28
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,062
I keep hearing about Git tags.

29
00:01:53,062 --> 00:01:54,874
What is it, really?

30
00:01:55,433 --> 00:02:00,302
A tag is a name fixed to one commit, usually a version:

31
00:02:00,302 --> 00:02:00,922
v1.0.0.

32
00:02:00,922 --> 00:02:05,348
Unlike a branch, it does not move when you commit.

33
00:02:05,348 --> 00:02:13,493
We create tags, list them, check one out, and push them (tags are not pushed with branches).

34
00:02:15,233 --> 00:02:19,633
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

35
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:26,188
Tag the current commit as the first release, and an older commit as a pre-release:

36
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:28,391
Work continues; the tag stays:

37
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:31,651
Work continues; the tag stays:

38
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:36,905
Look at the code of a release (a detached HEAD, lesson 78:

39
00:02:36,905 --> 00:02:38,808
look, do not commit):

40
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:44,612
Look at the code of a release (a detached HEAD, lesson 78:

41
00:02:44,612 --> 00:02:46,511
look, do not commit):

42
00:02:48,267 --> 00:02:49,856
Let me try something.

43
00:02:49,856 --> 00:02:53,641
Push the branch and expect the tags on the server:

44
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,232
git push sends branches, not tags.

45
00:02:57,232 --> 00:03:01,066
Grace, cloning or fetching from the server,

46
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:07,755
would not see v1.0.0, and a CI job triggered "on tag push" would never run.

47
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:16,480
(git push --tags would also push v0.9.0; push the tags you mean to publish.).

48
00:03:18,233 --> 00:03:18,947
Got it.

49
00:03:18,947 --> 00:03:21,698
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:27,538
A tag names a commit permanently; branches move, tags do not.

51
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:30,859
Tags must be pushed explicitly:

52
00:03:30,859 --> 00:03:32,640
git push origin TAG.

53
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:39,295
Check out a tag to inspect a release (detached HEAD); never move published tags.

54
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:43,668
How does annotated tags work?

55
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:46,282
Git has two kinds of tags.

56
00:03:46,282 --> 00:03:49,907
A lightweight tag is just a name for a commit.

57
00:03:49,907 --> 00:03:53,139
An annotated tag is an object of its own,

58
00:03:53,139 --> 00:03:59,128
with a tagger, a date and a message (and optionally a signature, lesson 91).

59
00:04:00,867 --> 00:04:05,266
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

60
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:08,900
The two kinds, side by side:

61
00:04:09,433 --> 00:04:11,523
The two kinds, side by side:

62
00:04:11,523 --> 00:04:18,313
git tag -n shows each tag's message (a lightweight tag shows the commit's subject instead).

63
00:04:20,067 --> 00:04:21,663
Let me try something.

64
00:04:21,663 --> 00:04:27,746
In a repository with only lightweight tags, the build script calls git describe:

65
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:30,853
No annotated tags can describe '…'.

66
00:04:30,853 --> 00:04:33,552
However, there were unannotated tags:

67
00:04:33,552 --> 00:04:34,427
try --tags.:

68
00:04:34,427 --> 00:04:38,002
git describe uses only annotated tags by default,

69
00:04:38,002 --> 00:04:42,597
because lightweight tags are often temporary, personal markers.

70
00:04:42,597 --> 00:04:47,120
The version a build reports must come from a real release tag.

71
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:56,516
Create the release as an annotated tag (or, if lightweight tags are intentional, git describe --tags):

72
00:04:58,267 --> 00:04:58,980
Got it.

73
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,731
What do I need to remember?

74
00:05:02,267 --> 00:05:09,466
Lightweight tag = a name; annotated tag = an object with tagger, date, message.

75
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,354
Use annotated (or signed) tags for releases; git describe relies on them.

76
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:20,369
git cat-file -t TAG tells the kinds apart.

77
00:05:22,133 --> 00:05:24,493
Why would I need Git bisect?

78
00:05:25,033 --> 00:05:29,798
git bisect finds the commit that introduced a bug by binary search:

79
00:05:29,798 --> 00:05:32,997
you mark a known good and a known bad commit,

80
00:05:32,997 --> 00:05:39,113
Git checks out the middle, you test, and it halves the range until one commit remains.

81
00:05:39,113 --> 00:05:43,308
Ten commits take about 3–4 tests; a thousand take about 10.

82
00:05:45,067 --> 00:05:49,466
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

83
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:50,803
The bug:

84
00:05:50,803 --> 00:05:54,916
an espresso and a latte should cost 5.70.

85
00:05:54,916 --> 00:05:57,524
The first commit was fine.

86
00:05:58,067 --> 00:06:00,100
The first commit was fine.

87
